    BEDTools.Bindings.window(; options...)::Cmd

Examines a "window" around each feature in A and reports all features in B that overlap the window. For each overlap the entire entry in A and B are reported.

The `bedtools window` command with `options`, checked against those that its help lists, to run with [`BEDTools.lines`](@ref) or any of Julia's process functions.
It needs `a` and `b`: `BEDTools.Bindings.window(a = …, b = …)`.
Its usage, in BEDTools 2.31.1, is `bedtools window [OPTIONS] -a <bed/gff/vcf> -b <bed/gff/vcf>`.

An option of `true` is a flag, `false` and `nothing` leave it out, a vector gives it each element as an argument, and another value gives it that value.
An option with several spellings, such as a short and a long form, takes any one of them, and each emits its own.

Options:
- `a`: `<bed/gff/vcf>`, from the usage line.
- `b`: `<bed/gff/vcf>`, from the usage line.
- `abam`: The A input file is in BAM format. Output will be BAM as well. Replaces -a.
- `ubam`: Write uncompressed BAM output. Default writes compressed BAM.
- `bed`: When using BAM input (-abam), write output as BED. The default is to write output in BAM when using -abam.
- `w`: Base pairs added upstream and downstream of each entry in A when searching for overlaps in B. - Creates symmetrical "windows" around A. - Default is 1000 bp. - (INTEGER)
- `l`: Base pairs added upstream (left of) of each entry in A when searching for overlaps in B. - Allows one to define asymmetrical "windows". - Default is 1000 bp. - (INTEGER)
- `r`: Base pairs added downstream (right of) of each entry in A when searching for overlaps in B. - Allows one to define asymmetrical "windows". - Default is 1000 bp. - (INTEGER)
- `sw`: Define -l and -r based on strand. For example if used, -l 500 for a negative-stranded feature will add 500 bp downstream. - Default = disabled.
- `sm`: Only report hits in B that overlap A on the _same_ strand. - By default, overlaps are reported without respect to strand.
- `Sm`: Only report hits in B that overlap A on the _opposite_ strand. - By default, overlaps are reported without respect to strand.
- `u`: Write the original A entry _once_ if _any_ overlaps found in B. - In other words, just report the fact >=1 hit was found.
- `c`: For each entry in A, report the number of overlaps with B. - Reports 0 for A entries that have no overlap with B. - Overlaps restricted by -w, -l, and -r.
- `v`: Only report those entries in A that have _no overlaps_ with B. - Similar to "grep -v."
- `header`: Print the header from the A file prior to results.

It also takes the general parameters of BEDTools, in [`BEDTools.Bindings.GENERAL_OPTIONS`](@ref), which come before the subcommand.
"""
function window(; options...)
    return build("window", WINDOW_OPTIONS, WINDOW_ALIASES, WINDOW_REQUIRED; options...)
end

